From: Piotr Kubik <piotr.kubik@adtran.com>
Date: Mon, 30 Mar 2026
Subject: [PATCH] leds: trigger: gpio: use GPIOD_FLAGS_BIT_NONEXCLUSIVE for trigger-sources

When a GPIO is shared between the LED trigger driver and another driver,
the LED trigger driver needs to request the GPIO with GPIOD_FLAGS_BIT_NONEXCLUSIVE to allow
both drivers to monitor the same GPIO pin.

Without this flag, if another driver has already claimed the GPIO, theLED trigger driver's
gpiod_get_optional() call fails silently, and the LED trigger doesn't work.

This is needed for scenarios like:
- SFP module presence LED triggered by SFP Mod_ABS
- SFP link status LED triggered by SFP Rx_LOS

Both GPIOs are also monitored by the SFP driver for module state
management, so they need to be shared.

Additionally add an optional "trigger-sources-inverted" device property
that lights the LED on logical 0. A nonexclusively shared descriptor
carries a single polarity (the first requester's), so a LED that must
show the opposite sense of the line (white "signal OK" while sfp reads
active-high LOS) cannot express that through GPIO flags alone.

Signed-off-by: Piotr Kubik <piotr.kubik@adtran.com>
---
 drivers/leds/trigger/ledtrig-gpio.c | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/leds/trigger/ledtrig-gpio.c
+++ b/drivers/leds/trigger/ledtrig-gpio.c
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
 #include <linux/init.h>
 #include <linux/gpio/consumer.h>
+#include <linux/property.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
 #include <linux/leds.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
@@ -19,6 +20,7 @@ struct gpio_trig_data {
 	struct led_classdev *led;
 	unsigned desired_brightness;	/* desired brightness when led is on */
 	struct gpio_desc *gpiod;	/* gpio that triggers the led */
+	bool inverted;			/* light the LED on logical 0 */
 };
 
 static irqreturn_t gpio_trig_irq(int irq, void *_led)
@@ -28,6 +30,8 @@ static irqreturn_t gpio_trig_irq(int irq
 	int tmp;
 
 	tmp = gpiod_get_value_cansleep(gpio_data->gpiod);
+	if (gpio_data->inverted)
+		tmp = !tmp;
 	if (tmp) {
 		if (gpio_data->desired_brightness)
 			led_set_brightness_nosleep(gpio_data->led,
@@ -86,7 +90,8 @@ static int gpio_trig_activate(struct led
 	 * The generic property "trigger-sources" is followed,
 	 * and we hope that this is a GPIO.
 	 */
-	gpio_data->gpiod = gpiod_get_optional(dev, "trigger-sources", GPIOD_IN);
+	gpio_data->gpiod = gpiod_get_optional(dev, "trigger-sources",
+					      GPIOD_IN | GPIOD_FLAGS_BIT_NONEXCLUSIVE);
 	if (IS_ERR(gpio_data->gpiod)) {
 		ret = PTR_ERR(gpio_data->gpiod);
 		kfree(gpio_data);
@@ -100,6 +105,13 @@ static int gpio_trig_activate(struct led
 
 	gpiod_set_consumer_name(gpio_data->gpiod, "led-trigger");
 
+	/* A GPIO shared nonexclusively with another consumer carries that
+	 * consumer's polarity; this property lets the trigger observe the
+	 * opposite sense of the line.
+	 */
+	gpio_data->inverted = device_property_read_bool(dev,
+						"trigger-sources-inverted");
+
 	gpio_data->led = led;
 	led_set_trigger_data(led, gpio_data);
 
